<h1>About</h1>
<p>Yin began as a senior capstone project for RIT's Digital Humanities and Social Sciences Bachelor's Program, conceived of by Leo Holman and created by him and Christine McCullough.</p>
<p>We know that learning Chinese is hard, especially for native English speakers. One of the reasons for this is because English speakers aren't used to paying attention to tone. The tone with which you say a word, such as "mother", may have some semantic meaning (she may think you're mad at her, or happy, depending on your tone), but there's no chance that the tone in which you say "mother" will ever make that word mean "horse." (For this example, see Lesson 1.)</p>
<p>One of the hardest parts of understanding tone in Chinese is learning how to hear it. In order to learn how to produce tone, you must first learn how to distinguish one from another. </p>
<p>As most English-speakers' ears aren't trained for this, we created Yin to shift the burden from auditory to visual senses. Rather than relying all on hearing the difference in tone, we thought it might first be helpful to see the difference. </p>
<p>In our graphing function, we plot the tone of your voice on a pitch curve alongside a native speaker's so that you can see where your curve differs. Perhaps you notice you're starting too low, or ending too high. Trying to get your graph to match the shape of the native speaker's pitch curve will train you to produce tones more accurately. Then, once you're able to see the differences in tone, perhaps you'll be able to hear them as well.</p>
<p>We are expanding Yin to include Yin Classroom, a new tool that will allow teachers to create lessons, vocabulary lists, and quizzes for their students. We know it is helpful for students to be able to practice the words they're learning in class, so Yin Classroom will feature all of the same activities only with whatever words the teacher selects.</p>
<p>We'd love to hear your feedback! Feel free to contact us with any thoughts, suggestions, problems, or concerns at <a href="mailto:yinwebapp@gmail.com">yinwebapp@gmail.com</a></p>
